// Grade 10 world history: six turns that made the modern world.
function GRADE10_HISTORY_MODULES() { return [
  {
    id: 'ancient-civilizations',
    order: 1,
    title: 'The first civilizations',
    tagline: 'Rivers, writing and law',
    lesson: {
      paragraphs: [
        "Civilization began where rivers flooded and farming fed more people than farming needed.\n[[Mesopotamia, between the Tigris and Euphrates, about 3500 BC: the first cities and the first writing.]]\n[[Egypt on the Nile, about 3100 BC: a kingdom that lasted three thousand years.]]\n[[The Indus Valley and China\'s Yellow River soon after.]]",
        "Let's look at what a surplus does. When one farmer can feed five people, four are free to do something else: build, trade, rule, pray, write. Writing began as accounting, marks on clay for grain and sheep, and became law, story and scripture. Hammurabi\'s code, about 1750 BC, is the first long set of written laws we have.",
        "Every civilization solved the same problems in its own way: how to feed a city, how to settle a quarrel, how to remember. When you meet a new one, ask those three questions first.",
      ],
      keyIdea: 'Civilization began on flooding rivers where a surplus freed people to build, rule and write; writing began as accounting and became law.',
      example: { kind: 'letters', text: 'rivers, surplus, writing', caption: 'How civilization starts.' },
    },
    sources: ['Aligned with Texas TEKS WH.2A (summarize the impact of the development of farming on the creation of river valley civilizations) and NCSS Theme II.'],
    generators: ['h10-which-river', 'h10-surplus', 'h10-period-year', 'h10-first-writing', 'h10-period-fact'],
  },
  {
    id: 'greece-and-rome',
    order: 2,
    title: 'Greece and Rome',
    tagline: 'Democracy, republic, empire',
    requires: ['ancient-civilizations'],
    lesson: {
      paragraphs: [
        "Athens invented democracy about 500 BC: citizens voting directly on laws, though citizens meant free men only. Rome, from 509 BC, was a republic: citizens electing officials and a senate that lasted almost five centuries.\n[[Athens: direct democracy. Rome: a republic with elected officials.]]",
        "Let's follow Rome\'s turn. Generals grew stronger than the senate. Julius Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BC and was killed in 44 BC; his heir Augustus became the first emperor in 27 BC. The republic was over and the empire lasted, in the west, until 476 AD.",
        "The founders of the United States read this history closely. Their fear of a Caesar is why the Constitution splits power three ways and limits terms. When you study Rome, you are reading the founders\' notes.",
      ],
      keyIdea: 'Athens invented direct democracy, Rome built a republic that became an empire in 27 BC, and the American founders designed against Rome\'s fall.',
      example: { kind: 'letters', text: '27 BC', caption: 'Augustus, the first emperor.' },
    },
    sources: ['Aligned with Texas TEKS WH.3B (compare the democracy of Athens and the republic of Rome) and NCSS Theme VI.'],
    generators: ['h10-athens-or-rome', 'h10-rome-year', 'h10-athens-or-rome', 'h10-rome-year', 'h10-founders-fear'],
  },
  {
    id: 'middle-ages-and-renaissance',
    order: 3,
    title: 'The Middle Ages and the Renaissance',
    tagline: 'Feudal Europe, the plague, and rebirth',
    requires: ['greece-and-rome'],
    lesson: {
      paragraphs: [
        "After Rome fell in the west, Europe broke into small kingdoms held together by feudalism: land for loyalty, lords and knights above, peasants below, and the church over all.\n[[Feudalism: land in exchange for service.]]\nIn 1347 the Black Death arrived and killed perhaps a third of Europe in five years.",
        "Let's look at the rebirth. With fewer workers, labor grew valuable and old bonds loosened. Trade cities in Italy grew rich, and from about 1400 the Renaissance rediscovered Greek and Roman learning and made new art and science on top of it. Gutenberg\'s printing press, about 1450, put books in ordinary hands.\n[[About 1450: the printing press.]]",
        "Printing did to the Middle Ages what the internet did to the twentieth century. Ideas moved faster than any ruler could stop them, and the next century, the Reformation and the age of exploration, followed straight from it.",
      ],
      keyIdea: 'Feudalism held Europe together after Rome; the Black Death of 1347 loosened it; the Renaissance and the printing press of about 1450 remade it.',
      example: { kind: 'letters', text: '1347, 1450', caption: 'The plague, then the press.' },
    },
    sources: ['Aligned with Texas TEKS WH.5A (explain the political, intellectual, artistic, economic, and religious impact of the Renaissance) and NCSS Theme II.'],
    generators: ['h10-medieval-year', 'h10-feudal', 'h10-medieval-year', 'h10-press', 'h10-feudal'],
  },
  {
    id: 'age-of-revolutions',
    order: 4,
    title: 'The age of revolutions',
    tagline: 'America, France and the idea of rights',
    requires: ['middle-ages-and-renaissance'],
    lesson: {
      paragraphs: [
        "In the 1600s and 1700s, thinkers of the Enlightenment argued that people have natural rights and that governments get their power from the governed. Two revolutions put that idea to the test.\n[[1776: the American Revolution declares it.]]\n[[1789: the French Revolution tries it, and by 1793 is executing its king and then its own leaders.]]",
        "Let's look at why they went differently. America was three thousand miles from its king and had elected assemblies for a century; the revolution replaced a distant government with a nearby one. France tore out a whole society at once, with no practice at self-rule, and the result was terror and then Napoleon, an emperor by 1804.",
        "The ideas survived both. Rights, consent, constitutions: by 1850 they had spread across Europe and Latin America, and the arguments of 1776 and 1789 are still the arguments of any country deciding who rules.",
      ],
      keyIdea: 'The Enlightenment said rights come first and governments from consent; America tested it in 1776 and France in 1789, one with practice at self-rule and one without.',
      example: { kind: 'letters', text: '1776, 1789', caption: 'Two revolutions.' },
    },
    sources: ['Aligned with Texas TEKS WH.9B (compare the causes, characteristics, and consequences of the American and French revolutions) and NCSS Theme VI.'],
    generators: ['h10-rev-year', 'h10-why-different', 'h10-rev-year', 'h10-enlightenment', 'h10-why-different'],
  },
  {
    id: 'industrial-revolution',
    order: 5,
    title: 'The Industrial Revolution',
    tagline: 'Steam, factories and cities',
    requires: ['age-of-revolutions'],
    lesson: {
      paragraphs: [
        "From about 1760 in Britain, machines driven by water and then steam began doing work that hands had done for all of history.\n[[The steam engine, improved by James Watt in the 1770s.]]\n[[Factories, railroads by the 1830s, and cities that doubled in a generation.]]",
        "Let's look at the price and the payoff. Children worked twelve-hour days in mills; cities were crowded and sick. And for the first time in history, ordinary people grew steadily richer, life expectancy rose, and a shirt cost a day\'s wage instead of a month\'s. Both things are true, and reformers spent the next century fixing the first without losing the second.",
        "Every later change, electricity, cars, computers, follows the same pattern: a new source of power, a burst of growth, real harm to real people, and laws that catch up. Knowing the pattern is the point of studying the first one.",
      ],
      keyIdea: 'Steam and factories from about 1760 made ordinary people richer for the first time in history, at a real cost that reformers spent a century fixing.',
      example: { kind: 'letters', text: 'steam, factories, cities', caption: 'The Industrial Revolution.' },
    },
    sources: ['Aligned with Texas TEKS WH.10A (identify the causes and effects of the Industrial Revolution) and NCSS Theme VIII (Science, Technology, and Society).'],
    generators: ['h10-industry-fact', 'h10-price-and-payoff', 'h10-industry-fact', 'h10-pattern', 'h10-price-and-payoff'],
  },
  {
    id: 'world-wars',
    order: 6,
    title: 'The world wars',
    tagline: '1914 to 1945',
    requires: ['industrial-revolution'],
    lesson: {
      paragraphs: [
        "In 1914 an assassination in Sarajevo pulled Europe\'s alliances into a war nobody planned. Four years of trenches killed about ten million soldiers. The peace of 1919 punished Germany and satisfied no one.\n[[1914 to 1918: the First World War.]]",
        "Let's follow the road to the second. Depression, resentment and fear brought Hitler to power in Germany in 1933. He invaded Poland in 1939, and the war spread across Europe, Asia and the Pacific. The United States entered after Pearl Harbor in December 1941. Germany surrendered in May 1945 and Japan in August, after two atomic bombs.\n[[1939 to 1945: the Second World War.]]",
        "About sixty million people died in the second war, most of them civilians, six million of them Jews murdered in the Holocaust. The United Nations, formed in 1945, was built on the promise never again. Studying the wars is how each generation is asked to keep it.",
      ],
      keyIdea: 'The First World War, 1914 to 1918, made the peace that led to the Second, 1939 to 1945.\nSixty million people died, six million of them Jews murdered in the Holocaust.\nThe United Nations was built in 1945 on the promise never again.',
      example: { kind: 'letters', text: '1914, 1939, 1945', caption: 'The world wars.' },
    },
    sources: ['Aligned with Texas TEKS WH.11A and WH.12B (identify the causes and effects of the world wars; explain the roles of major participants; and explain the Holocaust) and NCSS Theme IX (Global Connections).'],
    generators: ['h10-war-year', 'h10-war-fact', 'h10-war-year', 'h10-war-fact', 'h10-never-again'],
  },
]; }

