        "Let's look at what competition does.\nTwo pizza shops on one street. If one raises its price or lets its quality slip, customers walk to the other. So both keep prices down and quality up, without anyone ordering them to.\nNow imagine one shop and no other for a hundred miles. That is a **monopoly**. It can charge what it likes, and it does.\nThe Standard Oil breakup of 1911 was the government acting as referee. Competition had been crushed, so the law restored it.",
        "A business lives on **profit**.\n[[Revenue = price × quantity sold]]\n[[Profit = revenue − costs]]\nSell 200 pizzas at 12 dollars, and revenue is 2,400 dollars. If costs were 1,900, profit is 500.\nA loss sends a signal too: stop making this, and make something people want more. Profit and loss are how a free market tells producers what to do.",
